    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Why Is Câmara de Lobos Called the Chamber of Wolves?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>8 November 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Because the wolves were seals. Lobo-marinho, sea wolf, is the Portuguese name for the monk seal, and when João Gonçalves Zarco&#x27;s men rowed into this sheltered bay in 1419 they found it full of them, so they called it the chamber of the sea wolves.</p>

      <h2>What does Câmara de Lobos actually mean?</h2><p>Word for word, it means chamber of wolves. But nobody in the 15th century thought there were wolves on Madeira. The full Portuguese name of the animal is <em>lobo-marinho</em>, sea wolf, and it is still the everyday word in Portugal for the Mediterranean monk seal, <em>Monachus monachus</em>. The bay was named for seals, and the short form simply dropped the "marinho".</p><p>"Câmara" means chamber or room. Used for a stretch of coast, it describes exactly what the place is: a small, closed bay, protected by rock on both sides, where the sea goes quiet. In modern Portuguese the same word also means a town council, which is why the town hall here is the Câmara Municipal de Câmara de Lobos.</p><h2>Who named it, and when?</h2><p>The name comes from the first Portuguese exploration of Madeira's south coast. Madeira's own tradition dates the arrival of João Gonçalves Zarco and Tristão Vaz Teixeira to 1419, and the accounts of that first trip describe the crew moving west along the coast in small boats, naming places as they went.</p><p>The fullest version is told by Gaspar Frutuoso, a 16th century chronicler from the Azores, in his book <em>Saudades da Terra</em>. He writes that at this cove the men found <em>"tantos lobos-marinhos que era espanto"</em>, so many sea wolves it was astonishing, and that the captain named the calm cove Câmara de Lobos. He also records that the men killed many of the animals for sport. It is not a pretty detail, but it is the honest one, and it hints at how the colony disappeared.</p><p>Frutuoso wrote more than 150 years after the event, so treat the scene as a well-kept tradition rather than a ship's log. Even the year is debated: some historians put the first sight of Madeira in 1420 rather than 1419. What nobody disputes is the reason for the name.</p><h2>Did the explorers mistake the seals for wolves?</h2><p>No. Some travel sites say Zarco saw wolves, or confused seals with a wolf pack. That is a modern embellishment. Lobo-marinho was simply the word Portuguese sailors used for these seals, so the crew named the bay after the animal in front of them. Think of it like calling a sea lion a sea lion: nobody expects a lion.</p><h2>How much did the seals shape the town?</h2><p>More than most visitors realise. A few traces are still easy to find:</p><ol><li><strong>The family name.</strong> Zarco took the place name as his own. His descendants used "da Câmara", and his son is known to history as Simão Gonçalves da Câmara.</li><li><strong>The coat of arms.</strong> Zarco's arms showed two seals, and the town's crest today still carries two monk seals.</li><li><strong>The chapel by the boats.</strong> Tradition says Zarco ordered the small chapel of Nossa Senhora da Conceição on the bay in 1420. Historians cannot prove the date, and the building was reworked many times between 1723 and 1908, but it still stands beside the fishing boats.</li><li><strong>An early start.</strong> Zarco is said to have lived here in the first years of settlement, before Funchal took over, and Câmara de Lobos became a parish around 1430. It is one of the oldest on the island.</li></ol><h2>Are there still monk seals in Câmara de Lobos?</h2><p>No, not in the bay. Centuries of hunting and human pressure pushed the seals off the main island. The survivors hold on in the Desertas Islands, roughly 35 to 40 km south east of Funchal, where the land and the surrounding water have been protected since 1990 and a nature reserve since 1995.</p><p>Counts vary by source and year, but the Madeira population is a few dozen animals, not hundreds. The good news is that it has been growing slowly, and in 2023 the IUCN moved the Mediterranean monk seal from Endangered to Vulnerable. Now and then a seal is seen along the main island's coast. It is rare, and it is never something a boat trip can promise. If you want to know where the odds are real, read <a href="/posts/can-you-see-monk-seals-in-madeira">whether you can see monk seals in Madeira</a> and our <a href="/posts/desertas-islands-madeira-guide">Desertas Islands guide</a>.</p><h2>What does the bay look like from the sea today?</h2><p>Smaller than you expect, and busier. Coming from Funchal, the bay opens about 3.7 nautical miles west of the marina, after the Socorridos river mouth. From the water you see the whole thing at once: the harbour wall, the colourful wooden fishing boats called <em>xavelhas</em>, the chapel, the small square behind it and the houses stacked up the rock.</p><p>To the west, the coast starts climbing straight away towards Cabo Girão, less than 2 km further on. That is the view the seals had, minus the houses: a cove closed by rock, sheltered from the north east wind, with a pebble shore to haul out on. It is easy to see why a colony chose it.</p><p>Câmara de Lobos still lives from the sea. It is closely tied to fishing for espada, the deep water black scabbardfish, and you will often see men working on boats and nets in the harbour. On the cliff at the entrance of the town is the viewpoint where Winston Churchill set up his easel in January 1950. From the water you can pick it out above the bay.</p><h2>Can you see Câmara de Lobos on a boat trip from Funchal?</h2><p>Yes, it is the first real stop west of Funchal on most coastal trips, because it sits on the way to Cabo Girão. On the Chifbay <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> it comes first, before the cliff, Fajã dos Padres and Ribeira Brava. On the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> you pass it on the way out and again on the way back, when the lights in the houses start coming on.</p><p>A boat is honestly the best way to understand the name. From the street you see a pretty fishing town. From the water you see a chamber: a small room of rock with one open wall facing the sea. If you want to walk it afterwards, for poncha or a plate of espada, our <a href="/posts/camara-de-lobos-madeira-guide">Câmara de Lobos guide</a> covers the town on foot. On the water, Chifbay's skippers slow down in front of the bay so you can take it in before the cliffs begin.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Were there ever wolves on Madeira?</h3><p>No. When the Portuguese arrived, the only mammals on Madeira were bats and, in the sea, monk seals. The name refers to lobos-marinhos, the Portuguese word for monk seals.</p><h3>What is a monk seal called in Portuguese?</h3><p>Lobo-marinho, which means sea wolf. The species is the Mediterranean monk seal, Monachus monachus.</p><h3>Where do monk seals live in Madeira now?</h3><p>Mainly in the Desertas Islands, a protected nature reserve south east of Madeira. Sightings near the main island are rare.</p><h3>Is Câmara de Lobos older than Funchal?</h3><p>Tradition says Zarco lived at Câmara de Lobos in the first years of settlement, before Funchal became the main town. Both date from the 1420s.</p>

      <blockquote>The bay kept the name and lost the seals, which is the most Madeiran way history could have gone.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

